From 300de4c58fb1765a9b90b76892e594864455c339 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 02:02:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] ufs: exynos: add the host controller driver for
 Exynos9820

Nothing upstream drives the UFS controller of an Exynos SoC, so the storage
this board boots from cannot be reached.  This is the half of that work
which speaks to the link; the analogue half went in with the PHY driver.

The link is brought up in four steps, and at each one a table of attributes
has to be written.  Those tables come from the vendor's calibration layer
for this SoC, drivers/scsi/ufs/ufs-cal-9820.c, and each line names the line
it came from.  That layer mixes two kinds of work in one table: attributes
of the link, which go out over DME or straight into the UniPro window and
belong here, and registers of the PHY's analogue block, which belong to the
PHY driver.  So a table here carries an entry that hands over to the PHY,
standing exactly where the vendor's table has its own block of PHY writes,
which keeps the order the hardware sees.  One place makes that necessary
rather than merely tidy: the vendor brackets the post link block of PHY
writes with a pair of writes to a UniPro attribute, and only a table can
keep the hand-over between them.

Everything the link is told about time is computed from the rate of the
UniPro clock, so that rate is printed.  A wrong one does not fail, it
produces a link that does not work.

Three of the core's hooks take the first three steps.  The fourth has no
hook, because the core has nothing at all after the change of speed, so it
is taken once the probe returns.  That is also where the link is checked to
have really reached a high speed gear: when the core cannot work out the
best mode it says so, skips the change and still reports success, which
would otherwise leave storage running at the slowest speed with nothing in
the log to say so.

Every clock the hardware may gate away on its own is released for the whole
of bring-up, including the one that gates the register bus of the PHY.  The
vendor leaves that one out of its bulk mask and instead clears and restores
it around every single access to the PHY, which would need the PHY driver
to reach into this driver's window.  Mainline Linux puts the bit in the
bulk mask and clears it once, and so does this, which keeps the two drivers
apart.

The pre link table is written again on every retry of the link start-up,
because the core calls that hook from inside its retry loop.  Everything in
it survives being written twice; the lane count is read once and kept,
since on a second pass the link may already be partly up.

Hibernation, power management, the hardware encryption unit and the error
handling of a running system are all left out on purpose: a boot loader
reads storage and hands over.  The one ordering difference from the vendor,
which resets the storage device after programming the host rather than
before, is marked UNVERIFIED in the code.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/ufs/Kconfig      |  14 +
 drivers/ufs/Makefile     |   1 +
 drivers/ufs/ufs-exynos.c | 673 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/ufs/ufs-exynos.h |  87 +++++
 4 files changed, 775 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/ufs/ufs-exynos.c
 create mode 100644 drivers/ufs/ufs-exynos.h

